== Other Established Baselines

Two established anomaly-detection methods are considered alongside the DINOv2 baseline: PaDiM @padim and PatchCore @patchcore. Both approaches use pretrained convolutional neural networks to extract representations from normal training images, but differ substantially in how these representations are modelled and subsequently used for anomaly detection. Comparing their architectures with the DINOv2-based approach provides additional context for the choice of representation and anomaly-scoring strategy used in this work.

PaDiM, or Patch Distribution Modeling, extracts features from multiple levels of a pretrained CNN and models the normal representation independently at each spatial patch position. For every position $(i,j)$, features observed across the normal training images are represented using a multivariate Gaussian distribution. At inference, the Mahalanobis distance between a test patch embedding and its corresponding learned normal distribution is used as the anomaly score. This allows PaDiM to account for both the average normal representation and the covariance between feature dimensions, while combining information from different semantic levels of the CNN. Because only the learned Gaussian parameters must be retained after training, its inference memory requirements are independent of the number of original training images. The method achieves an AUROC of 97.1%, exceeding the selected DINOv2 baseline of 95.8%. Its distribution-based representation of normal data is also conceptually related to the local distribution modelling investigated later in this work.

