PatchCore instead preserves representative examples of normal patch representations within a memory bank. Features are extracted from intermediate layers of an ImageNet-pretrained CNN rather than relying on the deepest representation. The authors argue that intermediate features retain more local information while avoiding some of the task-specific bias associated with deeper ImageNet classification features. Local neighbourhood aggregation is additionally applied to increase the receptive field of each patch representation without reducing the spatial resolution of the feature map. Features from two adjacent intermediate feature hierarchies are then combined to construct the normal patch-feature memory bank. \
As storing every normal patch would result in substantial memory and inference costs, PatchCore reduces this memory bank using greedy coreset subsampling. Rather than selecting patches randomly, the coreset attempts to retain representative coverage of the original normal feature space. At inference, each test patch is compared with its nearest neighbour in the resulting memory bank, with large distances indicating representations that are poorly supported by the observed normal data. The image-level anomaly score is primarily determined by the most anomalous patch, with additional re-weighting based on the neighbourhood surrounding its closest memory-bank representation. PatchCore achieves an AUROC of 99.1%, substantially exceeding the DINOv2 baseline and approaching perfect image-level ranking performance on MVTec AD.

