Both approaches differ from the DINOv2 baseline used in this work. PaDiM and PatchCore primarily operate on local feature maps extracted from pretrained CNNs, whereas DINOv2 is a Vision Transformer that directly provides both local patch tokens and a global CLS representation. The baseline developed here performs image-level anomaly detection using the CLS embedding and models the normal representation using distance-based scoring methods such as Mahalanobis distance and KNN. PaDiM instead models a separate Gaussian distribution for each spatial feature position, while PatchCore retains a representative collection of normal local features and performs nearest-neighbour comparison against this memory.

Despite the stronger reported AUROC of these specialised approaches, DINOv2 provides several properties that are particularly useful for the objectives of this work. The availability of both patch and CLS representations allows the relationship between local anomaly information and the final global representation to be investigated directly, while the transformer architecture provides a basis for future experiments examining how attention influences this aggregation. DINOv2 also provides a general pretrained representation upon which alternative normal-space modelling approaches can be developed, allowing improvements beyond the initial distance-based baseline to be investigated while retaining access to the underlying embedding geometry.

There is also an important distinction in how representation efficiency is approached. PaDiM reduces embedding dimensionality through random dimension selection, which can preserve anomaly-detection performance while reducing computational requirements. However, retaining similar AUROC does not establish what representational information has been discarded. This is less important for the baseline comparison considered here, where final anomaly-detection performance is the primary concern, but becomes more relevant when considering the interpretability of the learned representation, which is a greater focus of this work. Understanding which properties of the representation contribute to anomaly detection, rather than only whether performance is retained after compression, therefore provides an additional motivation for examining the DINOv2 embedding space directly.